        text: "In the city of St. Augustine, on Florida's northeast coast, stands a giant stone fort shaped like a star. It is called the Castillo de San Marcos, which means \"Castle of Saint Mark\" in Spanish. It is the oldest masonry fort in the continental United States. Masonry means it is built of stone.\n\nSpain founded St. Augustine in 1565. For more than a hundred years, the Spanish protected the town with forts made of wood. But wooden forts could rot in the damp weather or burn. After pirates attacked the town in 1668, Spanish leaders decided they needed something much stronger.\n\nIn 1672, workers began building a new fort out of a special stone called coquina. Coquina is made of tiny seashells that have been pressed and cemented together over thousands of years. Workers cut the stone from quarries on nearby Anastasia Island and carried it across the water to the building site. It took more than twenty years to finish the main fort, in 1695.\n\nThe star shape had a purpose. Each corner, called a bastion, stuck out so that soldiers could see and fire along the walls, leaving attackers few places to hide.\n\nToday, visitors can walk across the drawbridge, explore the rooms where soldiers once lived, and look out from the top of the walls at the same bay that Spanish soldiers watched more than three hundred years ago."
